<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cornelis van Vollenhoven</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornelis van Vollenhoven was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> legal scholar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a> University who founded the systematic study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>, the customary law of the peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. He divided the archipelago into nineteen adat law areas and argued that colonial government should respect indigenous law rather than impose European codes. His school trained generations of colonial officials and shaped the plural legal system of the Indies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven.jpg" alt="Cornelis van Vollenhoven" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CornelisvanVollenhoven.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
